## Becket React Component Authoring (MDC Rules)

Purpose: This is the single source of truth for creating production‑ready components for the Becket React library built on PandaCSS. It unifies our coding style and folder structure, and sets strict rules for API design, accessibility, UX, styling, and Storybook coverage.

- Repo context: monorepo with `@becket-ui/react` (components) and `@becket-ui/tokens` (PandaCSS tokens/recipes).
- Tech assumptions: React 19, TypeScript 7, PandaCSS v1, Storybook 10.
- Styling: use PandaCSS recipes/patterns/tokens from `@becket-ui/tokens`.

### Golden rules
- **Publishable & product-agnostic**: Becket ships as a public library. Export only reusable primitives. Do **not** export consumer-product presets, className strings for specific dashboards, or domain-specific names.
- **Always WCAG 2.2 AA**: Every interactive primitive must be fully accessible — keyboard, name/role/value, focus, and ARIA only when native HTML is not enough. This is not optional and does not wait for a later “a11y pass.” Bar: WCAG 2.2 **AA** + APG for overlays/disclosure (Dialog, Drawer, Menu, Tabs, Tooltip, Switch). Not AAA. Native HTML is the default, not a substitute for the bar. Fix epic: [MAV-29](https://kolmena.atlassian.net/browse/MAV-29). Test gate: [MAV-45](https://kolmena.atlassian.net/browse/MAV-45). Do not claim “accessible” / WCAG until both are Done.
- **Consistent API**: Follow the `as` prop pattern, forward refs, and surface HTML props of the base element.
- **Use Panda recipes**: Variants and sizes live in `@becket-ui/tokens` recipes. Components consume recipes; they do not hardcode styles.
- **Colocation & clarity**: Keep files near their usage, name things clearly, reduce nesting, prefer inline logic, and avoid premature abstraction.

---

## Folder structure & file placement

- New UI components live in: `packages/react/src/components/ComponentName.tsx`
- Stories live next to components: `packages/react/src/components/ComponentName.stories.tsx`
- If a component needs assets (icons, etc.), colocate them in a same‑name folder:
  - `packages/react/src/components/ComponentName/ComponentName.tsx`
  - `packages/react/src/components/ComponentName/asset-name.svg`
- `packages/react/src/components/` is for React components only:
  - Files here should export a UI component (or tightly coupled component subcomponents) and their local Storybook stories.
  - Keep the folder focused on rendering/API surface, accessibility behavior, and component composition.
  - Do not place cross-component styling engines, token-mapping helpers, parsing helpers, or generic utility modules in this folder.
- `packages/react/src/helpers/` is for shared non-component logic:
  - Use this folder for reusable helpers consumed by multiple components (for example, shared gradient-border logic).
  - Helpers should not render JSX or define UI components; they should expose typed utilities, guards, mappings, and class/style generators.
  - If logic starts in one component but is reused (or expected to be reused) by another component, promote it to `src/helpers`.
  - This keeps component files small, avoids duplicated behavior, and centralizes design-system mechanics in one place.

Follow the “prefer ≥3 items per folder” and “prefer colocation” guidelines. Do not nest deeper than necessary.

---

## Naming, imports, and style basics

- File name equals the main export: `Button.tsx` exports `Button`.
- Imports order: 1) 3rd‑party, 2) outer modules, 3) same‑module relative imports.
- Do not mutate function args or arrays; prefer native language features over utility libs.
- Prefer descriptive names; avoid implied `get` in function names.
- Initialisms keep consistent case (e.g., `URL`, `appID`).

---

## Component API rules

- Use `forwardRef` and expose the underlying element ref.
- Provide an optional `as?: ElementType` prop for element substitution.
- Extend the intrinsic HTML props for the base element (`React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<'button'>`, etc.).
- Pull visual props from the recipe variant types, e.g. `ButtonVariantProps` from `@becket-ui/tokens/recipes`.
- Pass **only defined props** into the pattern (conditional spread); do not pass `undefined` for omitted props.
- No business logic in UI components; UI state is allowed when it directly affects visuals/interaction.

Component template (consumption of a Panda recipe):

```tsx
import { ElementType, forwardRef } from 'react';
import { someRecipe } from '@becket-ui/tokens/recipes';
import type { SomeRecipeVariantProps } from '@becket-ui/tokens/recipes';

type SomeProps = React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<'div'> &
  SomeRecipeVariantProps & { as?: ElementType };

export const SomeComponent = forwardRef<HTMLDivElement, SomeProps>(
  ({ className, as: Comp = 'div', ...props }, ref) => {
    return (
      <Comp
        ref={ref}
        {...props}
        className={someRecipe({ /* variant props from props */ })}
      />
    );
  }
);

SomeComponent.displayName = 'SomeComponent';
```

### Layout / pattern-based components (Flex, Stack): only pass defined props

When a component uses a Panda **pattern** (e.g. `flex`) with many optional props, pass into the pattern **only props that are actually defined**. Do not pass `undefined` for omitted props; use conditional spread so the recipe/pattern receives only the keys the consumer set.

- **Rule**: For each optional prop (e.g. `align`, `justify`, `wrap`, `border`), include it in the pattern call only when it is defined, e.g. `...(align && { align: ALIGN_MAP[align] })`, `...(wrap && { wrap })`.
- **Reason**: Keeps generated class names and style props predictable and avoids polluting the pattern with undefined values.

When the component API uses different values than the pattern (e.g. `between` → `space-between`), define a **variant map** (e.g. `JUSTIFY_MAP`, `ALIGN_MAP`) and use it when passing the single prop into the pattern.

- **Type extraction**: Derive option types from the pattern: `type FlexOptions = NonNullable<Parameters<typeof flex>[0]>`. Use this for `border` and other pattern-native props; extend with custom tokens (e.g. `GradientBorderToken`) where the API accepts more than the pattern.
- **ClassName merging**: Merge in a fixed order: recipe class, then optional helper-derived class (e.g. gradient border from `getGradientBorderClassName`), then consumer `className`. Use a small `mergeClassName(...)` helper that filters out falsy values and joins with a space.
- **Destructuring**: Destructure all component-specific props (with defaults where needed), then `className`, `style`, `...props`. Forward `...props` and `style` to the root element so HTML attributes and inline style are preserved; apply merged `className` to the root.

Reference implementation: `packages/react/src/components/Flex.tsx`.

